    "description": "Youth employment in Iran involves both access to a first job and the transition from education into sustained economic activity. Unemployment statistics describe young people actively participating in the labor market, while education and inactivity indicators reveal additional dimensions of exclusion.",
    "tags": ["Youth", "Employment", "Education", "Unemployment", "Labor market"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:28:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:28:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Youth Employment in Iran: Jobs, Education and Unemployment",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s youth labor market, annual unemployment estimates, transitions from education, and the limits of headline employment statistics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "understanding-youth-employment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Entering the labor market",
        "content": "For young people in Iran, finding work is part of a wider transition between education, job search and economic independence. The World Bank identifies persistent youth unemployment alongside low female labor-force participation as important features of the country’s labor market. Its analysis also shows why changes in the unemployment rate must be read together with participation: during the pandemic, employment losses were accompanied by withdrawal from job search rather than an equivalent increase in measured unemployment. [s3]\n\nThe international series presented here defines youth as people aged 15–24. Its denominator is the youth labor force: young people who are employed or unemployed. Unemployment means being without work while available for and seeking employment. Consequently, the rate does not describe the proportion of all young Iranians without jobs. A student outside the labor force is excluded from that denominator. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "annual-unemployment",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: youth unemployment, ages 15–24, 2011–2025",
          "dataset_id": "youth-unemployment",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["youth-unemployment-rate"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Percent of youth labor force",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The modeled rate reached its highest value within this displayed period in 2016. It subsequently declined overall, although the 2025 estimate was higher than those for 2023 and 2024. This series alone cannot establish whether changes resulted from hiring, participation changes, or both. [s1]"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "education-and-inactivity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Looking beyond unemployment",
        "content": "A complementary measure counts young people who are not in employment, education or training, often abbreviated as NEET. Unlike unemployment, this measure uses the youth population as its reference group and includes people outside the labor force. The World Bank’s human-capital brief explicitly tracks this dimension for Iran alongside youth literacy. Reading these indicators together helps distinguish educational participation from disengagement from both learning and paid work. [s2]\n\nThese categories answer different questions. Someone studying and not seeking employment can be outside the labor force without being NEET. Someone neither studying nor working may be NEET whether or not they are actively seeking a job. Neither category establishes an individual’s motivation or explains the circumstances behind their position. The distinction matters when interpreting public discussions that use “jobless youth” without specifying the population being counted. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "unequal-transitions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Unequal transitions and the meaning of improvement",
        "content": "Young women face a particularly important intersection of barriers. The World Bank’s analysis describes young women leaving job search and documents the broader influence of caregiving responsibilities on women’s labor-market withdrawal during the pandemic. These findings concern a specific historical disruption; they should not be treated as a complete explanation of every young woman’s employment trajectory. [s3]\n\nFor readers assessing progress, the useful question is therefore broader than whether unemployment has fallen. Employment, continued education, participation and the quality of available jobs need to be considered together. The annual estimates below provide a consistent national benchmark, but they do not identify provincial opportunities, distinguish graduates by subject, or establish whether a first job offers adequate earnings and protection. A national trend is a starting point for understanding these transitions, rather than a description of every young person’s experience. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
