    "description": "Informal work in Iran concerns gaps in the recognition and protection of economic activities and employment relationships. It extends beyond unregistered businesses: informal jobs can also exist within formal enterprises and households.",
    "tags": ["Informal employment", "Labor rights", "Social protection", "Rural livelihoods", "Employment statistics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:28:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:28:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Informal Work in Iran: Jobs, Enterprises and Protection",
      "description": "Understand informal employment in Iran, the distinction between jobs and enterprises, historical rural–urban differences, and statistical limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "jobs-and-enterprises",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A distinction between the job and the enterprise",
        "content": "Informality can describe an economic unit or an employment relationship. The International Labour Organization, or ILO, distinguishes the informal sector, which concerns enterprises, from informal employment, which concerns jobs. Its explanation of the earlier statistical framework includes unregistered or insufficiently protected paid work as well as certain unpaid work within income-producing enterprises. This distinction prevents “informal” from becoming a loose synonym for a particular occupation or workplace. [s1]\n\nFor Iran, the distinction has direct empirical importance. The World Bank’s Spring 2022 Iran Economic Monitor reports informal employment within formal enterprises as well as in informal enterprises and households. A business’s formal status therefore cannot, by itself, establish the protection attached to every job inside it. The report’s table separates the distribution of informal jobs across these settings from informality rates within population groups; those denominators should not be confused. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "classification-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Why enterprise status does not fully describe a job",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Informal employment\"] -->|\"Can occur within\"| B[\"Formal enterprises\"]\n  A -->|\"Can occur within\"| C[\"Informal enterprises\"]\n  A -->|\"Can occur within\"| D[\"Households\"]\n  E[\"Enterprise classification\"] -->|\"Describes\"| F[\"The economic unit\"]\n  G[\"Employment classification\"] -->|\"Describes\"| H[\"The job or work relationship\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows classify where informal employment can occur and distinguish the enterprise from the job. They do not represent movements of workers, employment shares or a test of an individual worker’s legal status.",
